@node Evil and Doom
@chapter Evil and Doom Emacs Compatibility
@cindex Evil
@cindex Doom Emacs
@cindex evil-snipe
@cindex intercept map

Most cmacs subsystems put a read-only ``console'' buffer in front of you
--- the transcribe and transcode queues, the imgedit canvas, the
vidstudio timeline, the CAD model viewer and sketcher, the calculator
menu, the gsurf panes, the gnuseye entity list, the video and audio
players.  All of them are driven by @emph{bare single-letter keys},
which is exactly the space Evil claims for its own commands.  Under
Evil, and therefore under Doom, a plain major-mode keymap never sees
those keys.

The fix lives in one place, @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-evil.el}, and every
affected mode is a one-line call into it.

@menu
* Evil Precedence::             Why an overriding map is not enough.
* Evil Shim::                   What cmacs-evil.el does, and its boundaries.
* Evil Covered Modes::          Which modes are intercepted, and which are not.
* Evil New Modes::              Adding a new single-key mode.
@end menu

@node Evil Precedence
@section Evil's Precedence Stack

Evil does not install its bindings in a major-mode map or a minor-mode
map.  It builds @code{evil-mode-map-alist} per buffer and puts it in
@code{emulation-mode-map-alists}, which Emacs consults @emph{before}
minor-mode maps and long before the major-mode map.  Within that alist,
@code{evil-state-keymaps} (evil-core.el) orders the maps like this,
highest precedence first:

@multitable @columnfractions .08 .40 .52
@headitem # @tab Layer @tab Installed with
@item 1 @tab intercept keymaps @tab @code{evil-make-intercept-map}
@item 2 @tab state local keymap @tab @code{evil-normal-state-local-map}
@item 3 @tab minor-mode state maps @tab @code{evil-define-minor-mode-key} (evil-snipe)
@item 4 @tab auxiliary keymaps @tab @code{evil-define-key} on a keymap
@item 5 @tab overriding keymaps @tab @code{evil-make-overriding-map}
@item 6 @tab state global keymap @tab @code{evil-normal-state-map} etc.
@end multitable

Two consequences drive everything below:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{evil-make-overriding-map} --- the obvious fix, and what most cmacs
modes used to do --- only beats layer 6.  It loses to @emph{every} Evil
add-on that binds keys per state.
@item
@code{evil-snipe}, which Doom enables by default, lives in layer 3.  It
owns @kbd{s}/@kbd{S} (normal @emph{and} motion state) through
@code{evil-snipe-local-mode-map}, and @kbd{f}/@kbd{F}/@kbd{t}/@kbd{T}
(motion state) through @code{evil-snipe-override-local-mode-map}.
@end itemize

So a cmacs queue buffer under Doom would run almost every key correctly
and then silently drop @kbd{s}, @kbd{S}, and --- whenever the buffer was
in motion state --- @kbd{f} and @kbd{T} as well.  In @emph{normal} state
the motion-state maps are appended after all the normal-state ones,
which is why @kbd{f} and @kbd{T} appeared to work there while @kbd{s}
and @kbd{S} did not; the failure was state-dependent, which is what made
it look arbitrary.

@node Evil Shim
@section What cmacs-evil.el Does

@code{cmacs-evil-setup-mode-map} copies a mode map's @emph{own} bindings
into the auxiliary keymap for each requested Evil state and marks that
keymap as an @emph{intercept} map --- layer 1, above everything Evil or
its add-ons can install.  The mode is also registered in
@code{evil-snipe-disabled-modes}, so snipe never turns on in a buffer
where its keys could not fire anyway (no stray @samp{snipe} lighter, no
@kbd{;}/@kbd{,} transient map).

The boundaries are deliberate:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Only keys the mode itself binds are promoted.}  Bindings
inherited from @code{special-mode-map} (@key{SPC}, @key{DEL}, @kbd{n},
@kbd{p}, ...) are excluded, and keys the mode does not bind fall through
to Evil untouched: @kbd{C-w}, @key{ESC}, @kbd{:}, @kbd{/}, digit
arguments and the rest behave exactly as in any other buffer.
@item
@strong{Only @code{normal} and @code{motion} state are intercepted}
(@code{cmacs-evil-intercept-states}).  Insert, visual, replace and Emacs
state are left alone.
@item
@strong{The Doom leader always wins.}  general.el installs
@code{general-maps-alist} ahead of @code{evil-mode-map-alist} in
@code{emulation-mode-map-alists}, so @key{SPC} reaches the leader even
against an intercept map.  A mode that binds @key{SPC} (the video and
audio players, the vidstudio transport) therefore keeps that key only in
vanilla Emacs; under Doom use the mode's alternate key or @kbd{M-x}.
@item
@strong{Your bindings still win.}  @code{evil-define-key} on the same
mode map writes into the very keymap that is marked as the intercept
map, so a config that runs after the mode's file loads overrides the
default:

@example
(with-eval-after-load 'cmacs-transcribe
  (evil-define-key 'normal cmacs-transcribe-mode-map "s" #'my-command))
@end example
@end itemize

The companion entry point @code{cmacs-evil-intercept-mode-map} does the
promotion @emph{without} copying, for a mode that already installs its
keys per state with @code{evil-define-key*} and wants exactly those
bindings kept (gsurf, gsurf-lite, gnuseye's panes).

Both are no-ops when Evil is absent, and defer their work with
@code{with-eval-after-load} when Evil has not loaded yet --- so a vanilla
Emacs build behaves as if none of this existed.

@node Evil Covered Modes
@section Which Modes Are Covered

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@headitem Mode @tab Treatment
@item @code{cmacs-transcribe-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-transcode-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-imgedit-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-vidstudio-mode} @tab intercept; @key{SPC} is the Doom leader
@item @code{cmacs-calculator-menu-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-calculator-chart-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-cad-model-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-cad-sketch-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-cad-feature-tree-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-video-mode} @tab intercept; @key{SPC} is the Doom leader
@item @code{cmacs-audio-mode} @tab intercept; @key{SPC} is the Doom leader
@item @code{cmacs-gnuseye-forecast-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-gsurf-dom-mode} + console @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-gsurf-bookmarks-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-gsurf-downloads-mode} @tab intercept (copied)
@item @code{cmacs-gsurf-mode} @tab intercept (existing aux maps kept)
@item @code{cmacs-gsurf-lite-mode} @tab intercept (existing aux maps)
@item @code{cmacs-gnuseye-list-mode} @tab intercept (existing aux maps)
@item @code{cmacs-gnuseye-layers-mode} @tab intercept (existing aux maps)
@item @code{cmacs-gnuseye-inspector-mode} @tab intercept (existing aux maps)
@end multitable

Three groups are deliberately @emph{not} intercepted:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Emacs-state buffers} --- the gnuseye globe viewport
(@code{cmacs-gnuseye-mode}) and the libregnum viewport/editor
(@code{cmacs-libregnum-mode}, @code{cmacs-libregnum-editor-mode}).  Evil
binds nothing in Emacs state, so their single keys already reach the
mode map; intercepting normal state would break @kbd{C-z} as the way
back to Evil.
@item
@strong{Panels that avoid the contested keys} --- the libregnum
outliner, palette, assets and inspector panes bind @kbd{g}, @kbd{m},
@kbd{r}, @kbd{D}, @key{RET} in motion/normal auxiliary maps and never
collide with a minor-mode map.
@item
@strong{Editable text buffers} --- @code{cmacs-cad-gcode-mode} derives
from @code{prog-mode} and holds real G-code text.  Its bare
@kbd{g}/@kbd{G}/@kbd{e} viewer keys are a pre-existing design wart there
(they shadow self-insert in vanilla Emacs too); promoting them over
Evil's @kbd{e} and @kbd{g} motions would trade one problem for a worse
one.
@end itemize

Modes derived from @code{tabulated-list-mode} are immune to evil-snipe
on their own (snipe ships @code{tabulated-list-mode} in its default
@code{evil-snipe-disabled-modes}) but are still shadowed by Evil's
@emph{state} maps, which is why the gsurf bookmark and download lists
are in the table above.

@node Evil New Modes
@section Adding a New Single-Key Mode

For a new read-only console mode whose keys are plain @code{define-key}
entries in the mode map:

@example
(require 'cmacs-evil)                   ;Evil/Doom keymap precedence

;; ... defvar the mode map, define-key its keys, define-derived-mode ...

(cmacs-evil-setup-mode-map my-mode-map 'my-mode)
@end example

For a mode that already installs per-state bindings with
@code{evil-define-key*}, keep them and promote instead:

@example
(cmacs-evil-intercept-mode-map my-mode-map 'my-mode)
@end example

Both take an optional third argument, the list of states (default
@code{normal} and @code{motion}).

@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-evil-tests.el} holds the regression tests.  The
pure ones run everywhere; the precedence ones need the real Evil, so
they skip unless it is on @code{load-path}.  To run them against a Doom
install --- including the sweep that walks every covered mode map with
evil-snipe forced on:

@example
src/emacs -Q --batch \
  -L lisp/cmacs -L test/cmacs \
  $(printf -- '-L %s ' ~/.config/emacs/.local/straight/build-*/evil@{,-snipe,-collection@}) \
  -l ert -l cmacs-evil-tests -f ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit
@end example

Loading Doom itself in batch does @emph{not} work as a test harness: its
@code{use-package} deferrals never fire without an interactive session,
so Evil and evil-snipe are never enabled and every key looks fine.
